<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Starocherkasskaya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cherkassk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Starocherkasskaya is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> village on the lower Don in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, known until 1805 as Cherkassk. From the mid-17th century it was the capital of the Don Cossack Host, built on an island exposed to spring floods. After repeated flooding the capital was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> in 1805; the village keeps its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> Resurrection Cathedral, built in 1706–1719.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/starocherkasskaya.jpg" alt="Starocherkasskaya" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matvyei Duglasovich · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starocherkassk-May_2005-Mathew_Dodson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Starocherkasskaya is a stanitsa in Aksaysky District, on the right bank of the Don River about 35 kilometres upstream from Rostov-on-Don. Its name means Old Cherkassk. The town grew up on an island of the Don later called Monastyrsky. The site suited a fortress and a military camp, but not a centre of government and trade: in spring the island was flooded, and the crowded wooden town suffered devastating fires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> fortress probably stood on the island before 1570, though the chronicles first mention it in 1593. About fifty years later it became the capital of the Don Cossacks, at first an independent entity between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the tsars, and later the Don Voisko Province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. In its heyday in the 18th century Cherkassk was a busy town with a strong fortress. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stepan Razin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yermak-timofeyevich">Yermak Timofeyevich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondraty-bulavin">Kondraty Bulavin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matvei-platov">Matvei Platov</a> all lived there, and Russian, Ottoman, Crimean and Cossack forces fought hard battles around it.</p><p>In 1805 Platov, as ataman of the province, moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a>, a city built for the purpose on a hill about 40 kilometres away. Most of the inhabitants followed, and what remained of the old town became the stanitsa of Starocherkasskaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village is now a museum, a tourist centre and the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery for men; the writer Michail Aleksandrovich Sholokhov did much to organise the museum. Its more than forty historical sites include the Resurrection Cathedral with its gilded wooden iconostasis and the chains said to have held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stepan Razin</a>, the Transfiguration Church of 1740, the Petropavlovsk Church of 1751, the ataman's estate, the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kondraty-bulavin">Kondraty Bulavin</a>, the ruins of the Annensky fortress of 1730 and the monastery of Our Lady of the Don.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Starocherkasskaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their capital was <b>Cherkassk</b>, on an island in the Don.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-cossack-host">Don Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
